{
  "id": "80640",
  "type": "Page",
  "tags": [],
  "title": "The Equilibrium Constant Kc",
  "content": {
    "bibrefs": [],
    "model": [
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "When a mixture of reactants and products of a reaction reaches equilibrium at a given temperature, its reaction quotient always has the same value. It is then referred to as the "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "equilibrium constant ("
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "K"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ")"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "The value of the reaction quotient ("
                      },
                      {
                        "em": true,
                        "text": "Q"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": ") when a system is at equilibrium, representing the ratio of the concentrations ("
                      },
                      {
                        "em": true,
                        "text": "K"
                      },
                      {
                        "sub": true,
                        "text": "c"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": ") or partial pressures ("
                      },
                      {
                        "em": true,
                        "text": "K"
                      },
                      {
                        "sub": true,
                        "text": "p"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": ") of the products to reactants, each raised to the power of their respective stoichiometric coefficients."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "173643051",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "1303440412",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": " for the reaction at that temperature. When the equilibrium constant is evaluated in terms of concentrations, it is noted as "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "K"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "c"
              },
              {
                "text": "."
              }
            ],
            "id": "y39wsybdpxniyyw",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "3oq03o2hgkvfi9s",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "zf5alxf3f54822e",
            "src": "https://d2xvti2irp4c7t.cloudfront.net/media/A3/A31F9808B584CB71B1FEA56DD0BCDE4E/green_line.png",
            "type": "img"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "w5hrh8rxxnopfbr",
                "text": ""
              }
            ],
            "id": "2urcvzu2x0pi1sm",
            "items": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": " "
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "612770802",
                    "type": "p"
                  },
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "For the general reversible reaction:"
                      }
                    ],
                    "type": "p"
                  },
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": " ",
                        "type": "text"
                      }
                    ],
                    "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
                    "src": "a\\mathrm A\\;+\\;b\\mathrm B\\;\\;\\rightleftharpoons\\;\\;c\\mathrm C\\;+\\;d\\mathrm D",
                    "subtype": "latex",
                    "type": "formula"
                  },
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "the reaction quotient always assumes the same value at equilibrium at a given temperature. This can be expressed as:"
                      }
                    ],
                    "type": "p"
                  },
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": " ",
                        "type": "text"
                      }
                    ],
                    "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
                    "src": "Q_{\\mathrm{c}}\\;\\mathrm{at}\\;\\mathrm{equilibrium}=K_{\\mathrm{c}}=\\frac{\\lbrack\\mathrm C\\rbrack^c\\lbrack\\mathrm D\\rbrack^d} {\\lbrack\\mathrm A\\rbrack^a\\lbrack\\mathrm B\\rbrack^b}",
                    "subtype": "latex",
                    "type": "formula"
                  },
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "This equation is a mathematical statement of the "
                      },
                      {
                        "children": [
                          {
                            "text": "law of mass action"
                          }
                        ],
                        "content": [
                          {
                            "children": [
                              {
                                "text": "A law stating that, at equilibrium, the ratio of the concentrations or partial pressures of the products to the reactants, each raised to the power of their respective stoichiometric coefficients, is constant and equal to the equilibrium constant ("
                              },
                              {
                                "em": true,
                                "text": "K"
                              },
                              {
                                "text": ") for a given reaction at a particular temperature."
                              }
                            ],
                            "id": "3400278147",
                            "type": "p"
                          }
                        ],
                        "id": "1394917482",
                        "trigger": "hover",
                        "type": "popup"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": ": When a reaction has attained equilibrium at a given temperature, the equilibrium constant for the reaction, "
                      },
                      {
                        "em": true,
                        "text": "K"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": ", always has the same value."
                      }
                    ],
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "type": "dd"
              }
            ],
            "title": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": " The Law of Mass Action"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "914236620",
                "type": "p"
              }
            ],
            "type": "dl"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "k477dir5gwhcifa",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "oq327w6up963oz1",
            "src": "https://d2xvti2irp4c7t.cloudfront.net/media/A3/A31F9808B584CB71B1FEA56DD0BCDE4E/green_line.png",
            "type": "img"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "The magnitude of an equilibrium constant reflects the position of a reaction when it reaches equilibrium. A large value of "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "K"
              },
              {
                "text": "—much greater than 1—indicates that equilibrium is attained only after the reactants have been largely converted into products. A small value of "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "K"
              },
              {
                "text": "—much less than 1—indicates that equilibrium is attained when only a small proportion of the reactants have been converted into products."
              }
            ],
            "id": "ihdoz2hiuas5p28",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Once a value of "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "K"
              },
              {
                "text": " is known for a reaction, it can be used to predict directional shifts when compared to the value of "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "Q"
              },
              {
                "text": ". A system that is not at equilibrium will proceed in the direction that establishes equilibrium. It is important to recognize that an equilibrium can be established starting from reactants or from products or from a mixture of both."
              }
            ],
            "id": "tlfpxrq4o3a6dp5",
            "type": "p"
          }
        ],
        "id": "2240697728",
        "selection": {
          "anchor": {
